// One payload route, drawn the way a mapper would draw it: four path points placed front
// to back, a checkpoint dropped roughly beside the track rather than on it, and the cart.
//
// The route runs along TrenchBroom's +Y, so the points land on Godot's +X at 0, 10, 20 and
// 30 units. The third point opens a rollback stretch, the second a rollforward one, and the
// last two carry neither — that third combination is the ratchet, and it is what a route
// with no flags at all gets, so it has to be reachable from a real build.
//
// NO POINT SETS `target`, and the `targetname` values are deliberately scrambled (2, 0, 3,
// 1 against a placement order of 0, 1, 2, 3). That is the test: the route has to come out in
// placement order, not in name order, and not in any order the names could produce. A
// name-ordered chain would lay the curve out as 10, 30, 0, 20 and fail every curve
// assertion in the verifier.
//
// The checkpoint is deliberately 3 Godot units off the track (96 map units along
// TrenchBroom's +X). It is placed where a mapper would actually drop it, which is the
// behaviour the entity exists to paper over: the built node must end up on the route.
//
// `vertical_offset` is authored in map units and on TrenchBroom's up axis, so the built
// payload proves both halves of the conversion at once: "0 0 128" has to arrive as
// Vector3(0, 4, 0).
